  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083206/assets/d8fcc410e057fcfee15df2e182cdc1cf593c1b1fcf09029366e920fe48f17409.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-6,6].$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{\u2033}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-4,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5,$ as shown above. The intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave down are\n\n$(-6,$\n{{field-1}}$)\\,$\nand \n$\\,(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "A function $g(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{\u2033}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{\u2033}(x),$ we can see that ${g}^{\u2033}(x)<0$ on the intervals $(-6,-4)$ and $(1,5).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave down on the intervals \n\n$$\n(-6,-4\\,)\\,\\text{and}\\,(\\,1,5\\,).\n$$",
